ymbols moving.","cohort":["G1G2"],"criteria":["G1G2"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.601,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: '_resolve_tracker_git_dir — the one tracker filesystem primitive every lock path anchors on.'","gitutil.py:66-69 docstring: 'Shared by fsck's diagnostic + repair paths, compact, bridge_repair, tracker_maintenance and this module's own lock handling. Pure filesystem: no git subprocess...'","6 command-layer importers found: bridge_repair.py:49, fsck.py:58, fsck_recover.py:20, fsck_repair.py:35, fsck_scan.py:46, tracker_maintenance.py:99"],"finding":"The plan moves _resolve_tracker_git_dir into the new 'git_locking' layer, but this symbol is a general tracker-filesystem primitive (not a lock-contention decision): its own docstring says it is 'Shared by fsck's diagnostic + repair paths, compact, bridge_repair, tracker_maintenance and this module's own lock handling', and grep confirms 6 command-layer callers (bridge_repair, fsck, fsck_recover, fsck_repair, fsck_scan, tracker_maintenance) use it for a plain git-dir lookup with no locking. Placing it in a module defined as 'DECIDES what happens when git's own locks are contended' inverts the layering that ticket b432-c9dc-c1b4-4a45 established by pulling this primitive INTO gitutil.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f77fe895361456fba","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan 'The seam' — cluster membership of _resolve_tracker_git_dir","norm_id":"n519e77f854209ad7","priority":0.099,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["fsck/bridge_repair/compact resolve a tracker git dir and now transitively depend on a locking-policy module for a pure filesystem lookup, re-creating the layering inversion b432-c9dc-c1b4-4a45 was created to remove."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Keep _resolve_tracker_git_dir (and _resolve_common_git_dir if genuinely lock-only) placement justified against its 6 non-lock consumers; if it must move, name the new module for the filesystem+lock cluster rather than lock-contention decisions, or leave the primitive in gitutil and have git_locking import it back.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Enumerate the target module for run_git_bounded, run_git_write, and _with_transient_fault_retry, and demonstrate the resulting gitutil<->git_locking import graph is actually acyclic (or revise the 'dependency runs one way' claim to match reality).","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'gitutil RUNS a git subprocess; the extracted layer DECIDES what happens when git's own locks are contended. The dependency runs one way (gitutil -> git_locking).'","Code: run_git_write (line 690) calls _with_index_lock_retry(...) and _with_transient_fault_retry(_bounded_once); _bounded_once calls run_git_bounded. If run_git_write stays and _with_index_lock_retry moves, gitutil imports git_locking; if _with_index_lock_retry (moved) needs run_git_bounded, git_locking imports gitutil — a cycle.","The plan's moved-cluster list names _with_index_lock_retry and both flocks but is silent on run_git_bounded / run_git_write / _with_transient_fault_retry placement."],"finding":"The plan asserts the split yields a one-way dependency (gitutil -> git_locking), but the actual call graph is bidirectional: the lock-policy functions moving to git_locking (_with_index_lock_retry, _store_git_op_lock, and the retry that wraps run_git_bounded) invoke the git-running primitives (run_git / run_git_bounded), while run_git_write — which 'RUNS a git subprocess' and would stay in gitutil per the stated seam — itself calls _with_index_lock_retry and _with_transient_fault_retry. The plan does not state where run_git_bounded, run_git_write, and _with_transient_fault_retry land, so it cannot substantiate the 'one way' claim; the split risks a circular import that the re-exports would only paper over.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa21f85088afbedd7","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"The seam section: 'The dependency runs one way (gitutil -> git_locking).'","norm_id":"n2d19dc6384f64f64","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:cited-reference-inaccurate","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Implementer moves _with_index_lock_retry to git_locking but leaves run_git_write in gitutil, producing a two-way import that only works because Python resolves it lazily or because re-exports hide it.","A future reader trusts the 'one-way' invariant and adds a git_locking->gitutil edge, creating a hard import cycle."],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2222,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"no","cited_reference_accurate":"no","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","pat